"The alone/without-help expression needs a reflexive after by.","C":"The plural first-person subject we takes **ourselves**.","D":"Myself is singular and matches I, not we."}}},{"id":"b9731562-786c-5a2f-a32f-12902f889fa4","topicSlug":"reflexive-reciprocal","level":"B1","prompt":"“The two assistants helped each other to repair the rowing boat.” What does “each other” show?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Only one assistant received help."},{"id":"B","text":"Neither assistant received help."},{"id":"C","text":"Each assistant helped the other assistant."},{"id":"D","text":"Each assistant helped only themselves."}],"answerId":"C","hint":"A reciprocal action goes **from A to B and from B to A**.","explanation":"**Each other** expresses a reciprocal action between the two assistants.","keywords":["reciprocal-meaning"],"skill":"reciprocal-meaning","contextKey":"reflexive-reciprocal-50","fingerprint":"be53cbc02dbe880aedff754a8dda0398c46020be2de932c9a318abf66f788136","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-reflexive"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A reciprocal action goes **from A to B and from B to A**.","**Each other** expresses a reciprocal action between the two assistants.","Choose **Each assistant helped the other assistant.**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The stated reciprocal relationship goes both ways.","B":"This contradicts helped each other.","C":"**Each other** expresses a reciprocal action between the two assistants.","D":"That would be reflexive, not reciprocal."}}},{"id":"7c81d6f6-6c2b-51b7-aaa5-c8f7e49716cf","topicSlug":"reflexive-reciprocal","level":"B1","prompt":"“The supervisor asked Sam and ____ to repair the rowing boat.” Choose the standard object pronoun for the speaker.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"I"},{"id":"B","text":"myself"},{"id":"C","text":"mine"},{"id":"D","text":"me"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Test the role without the other name: **asked me**.","explanation":"Sam and **me** is the object of asked; removing Sam and leaves asked me.","keywords":["ordinary-object-pronoun"],"skill":"ordinary-object-pronoun","contextKey":"reflexive-reciprocal-50","fingerprint":"48cf91725f863d1c757ddc989c5abf26cf378d3bb4afedbdf67f8dce5841c843","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-reflexive"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Test the role without the other name: **asked me**.","Sam and **me** is the object of asked; removing Sam and leaves asked me.","Choose **me**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"I is a subject pronoun, but the phrase is the object of asked.","B":"The subject is the supervisor, so myself has no matching subject for an ordinary reflexive use.","C":"Mine is possessive and cannot be the person object here.","D":"Sam and **me** is the object of asked; removing Sam and leaves asked me."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Distinguish doing something to yourself, doing it alone and doing it to one another.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"The same participant twice","explanation":"A reflexive pronoun can be used when the subject and object refer to the same person or thing. Its form matches the subject: myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, yourselves, themselves.","pattern":"She introduced **herself**.","commonMistakes":["Her and herself are not interchangeable: her normally refers to someone other than the subject in this example."],"examples":["“She prepared the visitor guide by **herself**.” Choose the matching reflexive.","“She restored the old portrait by **herself**.” Choose the matching reflexive."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Emphasis and independence","explanation":"A reflexive can emphasise that the subject personally did the action. **By + reflexive** usually means alone or without help. **For + reflexive** means for one’s own benefit. These meanings are related but not identical.","pattern":"I made it **myself**. / I did it **by myself**.","commonMistakes":["By myself says without company or help; for myself says who benefits."],"examples":["“We prepared the visitor guide by **ourselves**.”","“We restored the old portrait by **ourselves**.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Reciprocal actions","explanation":"Each other and one another express a reciprocal relationship: A acts on B and B acts on A. They are not equivalent to themselves, where each participant’s action returns to that same participant.","pattern":"They introduced **each other**.","commonMistakes":["They looked at themselves means each looked at their own reflection; each other means they looked across at one another."],"examples":["“The two assistants helped each other to prepare the visitor guide.” What does “each other” show? → **Each assistant helped the other assistant.**","“The two assistants helped each other to restore the old portrait.” What does “each other” show? → **Each assistant helped the other assistant.**"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Use a reflexive only when its role needs one","explanation":"Ordinary English often omits reflexives with routine personal-care verbs: she washed and dressed. A reflexive is not a generally more polite substitute for me or I. In a coordinated subject, use I in formal standard prose.","pattern":"Please contact Sam or **me**.","commonMistakes":["Please contact myself is not the ordinary formal choice when no matching subject creates a reflexive relationship."],"examples":["“The supervisor asked Sam and **me** to prepare the visitor guide.” Choose the standard object pronoun for the speaker.","“The supervisor asked Sam and **me** to restore the old portrait.” Choose the standard object pronoun for the speaker."],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["She introduced **herself**.","I made it **myself**. / I did it **by myself**.","They introduced **each other**.","Please contact Sam or **me**."]},"stage":"5 · Complex sentences","prerequisites":["question-tags"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
